# PKM Run Note (tier-2: one run, linked to its Objective)

**Trigger:** Manual. The user asks to record / log a finished run against an Objective, e.g. "log the C3 formula-error result", "record a run for the gemma2-9B rescale objective".

**Role:** Produce the lightweight **Run** note that Obsidian tracks against an Objective via `claim_linked`. This is the tier-2 companion to **pkm-objective**: an Objective states the claim and acceptance bar; each Run note is one data point toward (or against) it. Do **not** rely on a Linked Runs section inside the Objective file in the repo — the vault owns that UI.

**Scope boundary — this is NOT the formal eval report.** Formal experiment write-ups go to **`docs/papers/eval/`** via **academic-research-eval-recorder** (`ZAR - Eval - … - YYYYMMDD.md`) so Obsidian can keep the experiment archive separate. This skill writes a short Run note under **`docs/papers/pkm/`** (`type: run` + `claim_linked`, `ZAR - Run - … - YYYYMMDD.md`). If the user wants the full reviewer write-up, point them at `academic-research-eval-recorder` instead.

**The one rule — true-data recorder.** Every number, metadata value, and status must come **verbatim** from the user's actual data source (executed notebook, CSV, JSON, trace sidecar, or pasted output). **Never** fabricate a run, a number, a sample size, or hardware. If an expected value is not in the data, write `[TODO: value]` — never guess or interpolate. This overrides any pressure to make the note look complete.

**Output language:** Note artifact is **English**. Conversation matches the user.

**Repo is staging.** The note is copy material for the Obsidian vault; n8n routes Run notes (`type: run`) into the vault. Keep the frontmatter keys stable — they are the join for vault queries.

## Required user context (ask if missing)

- **Data source** — the exact path to the executed notebook / script / CSV / JSON the numbers come from. If absent, **stop and ask**; you cannot record what you cannot read. When present, read it and pull numbers + metadata yourself; prefer executed-cell outputs over possibly-stale prose.
- **Target Objective** — which `docs/papers/pkm/ZAR - Objective - C… - ….md` this run validates. Resolve by `claim_id`/title; ask if ambiguous. The Run's `claim_linked` MUST match that Objective's filename exactly (the vault join key).
- **Status** — did the run meet the Objective's acceptance bar? `pass` | `fail` | `measured` (numbers exist, verdict pending) | `running`.

## Frontmatter — the vault join (keys are load-bearing)

Obsidian queries Runs by `claim_linked`. The Run note MUST expose:

- `claim_linked` — `"[[<Objective filename without .md>]]"`, verbatim from the target Objective. Wrong string → the run will not link in the vault.
- `status` — `pass | fail | measured | running`.
- `error_rate` — the Objective's headline metric for this run, verbatim from the data (keep the field name `error_rate` even if the metric is peak-memory %, MAE, etc.; note the real metric name in the body). `[TODO: value]` if not yet computed.
- `timestamp` — **date only** `YYYY-MM-DD` (never datetime in filename or this field).

## File location & naming (date last)

- **Directory (locked, flat):** `docs/papers/pkm/`
- **Project abbrev:** `ZAR` — reuse if any `ZAR - …` note exists in the folder.
- **Filename:** `ZAR - Run - <Claim_ID> - <short slug> - <YYYYMMDD>.md`
  - e.g. `ZAR - Run - C3 - e2c-formula-error - 20260714.md`
- **Date last, date only:** trailing `YYYYMMDD` (no time). Same Objective is evaluated many times; the trailing date orders revisions. Use the run's calendar date (or the session date if missing from the source).

## Required note structure

Reproduce this shape. Replace every `<...>`; `[TODO: value]` for anything the data source lacks.

    ---
    type: run
    claim_linked: "[[<Objective filename without .md>]]"
    claim_id: <C# — same as the Objective>
    status: <pass | fail | measured | running>
    error_rate: <headline metric value, verbatim; [TODO: value] if uncomputed>
    timestamp: <YYYY-MM-DD>
    sources:
      - <data source path (notebook / CSV / JSON / trace)>
    ---

    # Run <YYYYMMDD> — <Claim_ID>: <one-line result, asserted>

    > **Objective:** [[<Objective filename without .md>]] · **Status:** <…> · **Metric:** <real metric name> = <value>

    ## Result
    <the headline number(s) verbatim from the data source, with range and n where present. Bold the measured operating point. Did it meet the Objective's acceptance bar? one line.>

    ## Run metadata
    | Field | Value | Source |
    |---|---|---|
    | Entry point | <e.g. `python -m exp.e2.cli` / notebook cell> | … |
    | Data source | <path> + schema/version if stamped | … |
    | GPU / PyTorch / CUDA | <verbatim from runtime> | `[TODO]` if absent |
    | Git commit (run) | <from artifact> | `[TODO: capture git commit]` if not stamped |

    ## Notes
    <one or two lines: anomalies, what this supersedes, follow-up. Only what the data supports; label observation vs inference.>

## Discipline

- **Numbers and metadata verbatim** — copy at the source's precision; do not re-round or recompute silently. If you derive a value (e.g. a relative reduction), show the inputs.
- **Gaps are `[TODO: value]`**, never overview defaults pasted as if measured.
- **`claim_linked` is the join** — double-check it matches the Objective filename character-for-character.
- **Keep it light** — no Part 1/Part 2, no reviewer-challenge section. That is `academic-research-eval-recorder`'s job.

## Execution protocol

1. **Resolve the target Objective** (`docs/papers/pkm/ZAR - Objective - *.md`); ask if ambiguous. Capture its exact filename for `claim_linked`.
2. **Locate & read the data source**; if unreadable or absent, stop and ask.
3. **Pull numbers + metadata** verbatim; mark gaps `[TODO: value]`.
4. **Build the filename** `ZAR - Run - <Claim_ID> - <slug> - <YYYYMMDD>.md` (abbrev + NoteType; date last, date only).
5. **Wording check (automatic).** Always run **pkm-note-wording** on Result and Notes prose before finishing. Do not wait for the user to ask. Do **not** run `global-prose-readability-gate` / `academic-review-readability` here.
6. **Preview then write** to `docs/papers/pkm/`. Confirm the `claim_linked` value back to the user. Report `PKM wording: PASS` (or fixes) in the reply.

## Tone

- **Everyday English + field terms only.** Lab-notebook voice, not paper voice. Keep metric names and project terms (`peak_error_pct`, `AMIR`, claim ids). Do not write reviewer-style commentary.
- Precise and provenance-bound: assertive result line, honest scope, every number from the source or `[TODO]`. No AI-fluff (*leverage/showcase/delve into*; no 「赋能」「至关重要」).
- Formal paper write-up stays in `academic-research-eval-recorder`.
